  * The GPU shader cache (/var/tmp/.../m2v) needs a writable file system with free space, but the
  * root volume is deliberately not grown (a grown volume makes the iOS data migration start and
  * never finish). Instead deploy writes a second, small empty HFS+ volume into a pinned userdata
- * region; sweet_ufs exposes it as /dev/ufscache0 (0272) and this file mounts it at
- * /private/var/tmp before launchd starts.
+ * region; sweet_ufs exposes it as /dev/ufscache0 (0272) and this file mounts it before launchd starts.
+ *
+ * 0350: the volume is mounted at /private/var, not /private/var/tmp, and the data volume skeleton
+ * the image used to provide is recreated on it (170 directories taken from the image). Measured
+ * cause on this port: the root volume starts at 96.8 % used and /private/var is nearly empty in the
+ * image, so the whole data volume is built at run time (~200 MiB per cold boot) and the root fills
+ * up; at that fill securityd could not open its keychain database (EMFILE + ENOSPC in the same
+ * millisecond) and the user session collapsed.
+ *
+ * 0351: the skeleton must also carry the OWNERSHIP and MODE the image gave those directories. A
+ * directory created by the kernel is uid 0 / gid 20 / 0755, so /var/mobile/Library/Caches ended up
+ * root-owned and SpringBoard (running as uid 501) could not create its caches: the boot stalled at
+ * the Apple logo with 127 x NSCocoaErrorDomain 513 (NSFileWriteNoPermission) and backboardd
+ * "Sustaining the boot logo". The attributes are copied from the root volume, which still has the
+ * image's tree, BEFORE mounting, because the mount hides it afterwards.
  *
  * Boot-arg pandora_cachevol=1 enables it (default off, so a bad volume can never stop the boot).
+ * Boot-arg pandora_varcache=0 keeps the mount but skips the skeleton and its attributes.
  */
